---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const inlaws = [
  ["Crisci", 113, "Andreana Crisci married Vincenzo Falco. The commonest in-law name in the registers."],
  ["Morgillo", 76, "Angela Rosa Morgillo, daughter of Orazio, married Raffaele Falco."],
  ["Martone", 49, "Anna Martone married Aniello Maione; their daughter Costanza married into the Falco line."],
  ["de Lucia", 40, "Repeatedly beside Falco on the same register pages."],
  ["Rivetti", 17, "Chiara Rivetti, wife of Pasquale Falco."],
  ["Arricale", 15, "Giovanna Arricole, wife of Carmine Antonio Falco. Spelled Arricale in the registers."],
  ["Maione / Majone", 9, "Aniello Maione, father of Costanza. The register clerk used both spellings."],
  ["Ferrara", 3, "Also the family of Giuseppa Ferrara, who married Angelo Zampiello at Arpaia."],
  ["Falcone", 2, "Two glancing mentions only — which is the problem with generation 1."],
];
const books = [
  ["Baptisms", "1672–1738, 1761–1837", "Collegiate Church of Sant'Andrea Apostolo"],
  ["Marriages", "1754–1900", "with marriage supplements and proclamations into the 1920s"],
  ["Deaths", "1733–1888", "bound with the marriage series"],
];
---
<Base title="Arienzo">
  <h1>Arienzo</h1>
  <p class="lede">A comune of some six thousand people in the province of Caserta, at the western mouth of
  the Valle Caudina. For at least two centuries it was the whole world of this family.</p>

  <h2>What survives</h2>
  <p>The parish registers of the Collegiate Church of <strong>Sant'Andrea Apostolo</strong> are digitised
  and, since FamilySearch applied handwriting recognition to them, searchable by name rather than only
  page by page.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Series</th><th>Years covered</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{books.map(([a,b,c]) => <tr><td><strong>{a}</strong></td><td class="mono">{b}</td><td>{c}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small">There is a gap in the baptism series between 1738 and 1761. Civil registration begins
  separately in 1809, when the Kingdom of Naples adopted the Napoleonic system.</p>

  <h2>A family that married its neighbours</h2>
  <p>Three hundred and sixty-one pages of the Arienzo baptism registers mention a Falco. Reading them for
  the other surnames on the same pages gives the shape of the family's world — and every in-law name in
  the direct line is there, over and over.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Surname</th><th>Pages</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{inlaws.map(([n,c,w]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td class="mono">{c}</td><td>{w}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small">Counts are of register pages in which the name appears anywhere, not of individuals.
  The handwriting recognition is imperfect on 18th-century Latin hands — <em>Crisci</em> is also read as
  <em>Crisei</em>, <em>Morgillo</em> as <em>Morgitto</em> — and the counts above merge those variants.</p>

  <h2>The couple at the frontier</h2>
  <p>The oldest members of this line who can be shown to exist are
  <strong>Pasquale Falco</strong> and <strong>Chiara Rivetti</strong>. They appear together, named as a
  married couple of the parish, in the baptisms of their children:</p>
  <blockquote>
    …baptizavit infantem natam ex <strong>Paschale Falco</strong>, et <strong>Clara Rivetta</strong>
    coniugibus huius Parochiae, cui dedit nomen Maria Raphaela.
    <span class="small">— Arienzo baptisms, 24 February 1794</span>
  </blockquote>
  <p>Further entries for the same couple fall in 1795, twice in 1799 (a son, Domenico) and in
  February 1802 (a daughter, Maria Angela). The parish is named in one of them in full: the
  <em>insignis archipresbyteralis collegiata ecclesia Sancti Andreae</em> of Arienzo.</p>
  <p class="small">A note of caution. The family tree gives Pasquale's birth as 1765 and Chiara's as 1772.
  One further entry for the couple appears to read 1780, which cannot be right if those birth years are —
  Chiara would have been eight. Either the date or the birth years are wrong. The digits are the least
  reliable part of a machine reading of an 18th-century hand, and this needs a human eye on the page.</p>
</Base>
